WEBVTT

1
00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:15.000
You communicate nicely among yourselves, right?

2
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:21.000
Well, rules have to be followed, although we haven't really agreed on those corona rules very well, I don't know if you agree.

3
00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:27.000
For a start we'd have to agree that there is only one greeting during corona.

4
00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:32.000
Any one at all, because the other day a man held out his elbow to me and I hit his elbow with my fist,

5
00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:38.000
and then he held out his hand and I shoved my elbow into his hand, and in the end we groped each other more than if we had just shaken hands.

6
00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:46.000
So it would be good to agree on one greeting and that's it.

7
00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:02.000
I hate two things: I hate being woken up in the morning and I hate Instagram motivational speakers.

8
00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:03.500
It drives me crazy.

9
00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:10.000
Now I'll tell you a secret: for every ten hot women on Instagram I add some random idiot,

10
00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:13.000
so that when my wife tells me, you only follow women, I can say: no, no, no, no.

11
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:41.000
I remember one motivational message, I rephrase it a bit to suit me. I say: Tamara, do you know who Betty Robinson is?

12
00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:48.000
She says: I don't know. I say: you don't know who Betty Robinson is? Of course I didn't know either until two days ago, but I like to hint to her that there's something she doesn't know.

13
00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:53.000
Do you know who Betty Robinson is? You don't know who Betty is. Now I'll explain.

14
00:01:53.000 --> 00:02:00.000
Betty Robinson was some athlete from long ago, she ran, she won medals, and then she had a car accident and was in a coma for seven months.

15
00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:05.000
And when she woke up from the coma, she went back to athletics and won gold.

16
00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:08.500
And I tell my wife: you see how important it is for a person to get some sleep.

17
00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:14.000
You know, she just said: what have I gotten myself into.

18
00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:17.000
Because my wife isn't exactly a fan of dark humor.

19
00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:21.000
I constantly test the jokes I write for television on her and she doesn't like it one bit,

20
00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:25.000
because she has a really strong empathy, or, as I call it, being a crybaby.

21
00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:29.000
and there was a news story: the tree man died.

22
00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:34.000
Have you heard of that guy? Some dude who had a growth on his body, and over time it spread,

23
00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:37.000
like tree bark, so he went to a doctor and the doctor said:

24
00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:42.000
tree man, I guess, how should I know. I don't know when we got to these branches with medicine,

25
00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:45.000
naming diseases after what they look like.

26
00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:54.000
And the tree man died, and I tell my Tamara: well, a silver lining, right before the heating season.

27
00:02:54.500 --> 00:02:58.000
Which she didn't really like.

28
00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:08.000
She says: how can you? So you're not sorry? I said: it's really a pity, a young man, 42 years old.

29
00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:16.000
And then she blows a fuse and starts yelling: Srđan, what kind of man are you, how would you feel if you got that, would you be upset? I said: no,

30
00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:41.000
It's incredible what I read in the papers, let's talk about that.

31
00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:45.000
In the morning I get up and flip through the paper with my morning coffee,

32
00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:49.000
first news item: in the coffee we drink, we drink feces and poison.

33
00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:51.500
And great, never mind, I take a sip.

34
00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:57.000
And they constantly scare us with some marvels. Always some cataclysm, some catastrophe.

35
00:03:57.000 --> 00:04:03.000
A few years ago there was a headline: Western Serbia shackled by 20 centimeters of snow.

36
00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:10.000
How much is 20 centimeters? You women, you don't have to lie.

37
00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:18.000
But how much is 20 centimeters? Can that shackle anything? Literally an amount of snow that you can't go outside in house slippers.

38
00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:21.000
Well, that's the level of snow. But there is good news too.

39
00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:25.000
It says: charred corpse found. I mean, that's not good news.

40
00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:34.000
Charred corpse found, police suspect that the victim set herself on fire or that someone set her on fire.

41
00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:49.000
It says: how do I stop my son from carrying a bomb to school? Really, how do you stop a kid with a bomb?

42
00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:09.000
And schools already look like war zones lately: kids fight, shove umbrellas up each other's behinds, insult each other.

43
00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:15.000
And the state doesn't try to solve it by passing some law against peer violence, an Aleksa's law or something like that.

44
00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:18.000
No, they hold meetings and always have great ideas on how to solve it.

45
00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:23.000
At one point they proposed introducing uniforms, because sooner or later someone will say:

46
00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:27.000
hey, don't hit me, we're dressed the same. It's a shame to hit when we're dressed the same.

47
00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:39.500
Kids skip classes.

48
00:05:39.500 --> 00:05:44.000
God, imagine how bored the kids are: everything is a swipe away, every piece of information,

49
00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:47.000
then you come and drone on at them for 45 minutes in class and they run away, of course.

50
00:05:47.000 --> 00:05:54.000
But then they made a proposal for how to stop kids skipping classes: the parent will pay a fine of 1500 euros.

51
00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:58.000
That's a great idea, so we'll have parent squads in front of the school

52
00:05:58.000 --> 00:06:01.000
with nets and spears.

53
00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:11.000
I don't need you alive for 1500 euros at all. I don't need you alive.

54
00:09:14.000 --> 00:09:18.000
Slavica Đukić Dejanović, she deals with population policy,

55
00:09:18.000 --> 00:09:25.000
stated: people are less and less often deciding to have a first child.

56
00:09:25.000 --> 00:09:27.500
Let's start with the second one.

57
00:09:28.000 --> 00:09:33.000
You get to the maternity ward, they say: congratulations, you've had a second child.

58
00:09:33.000 --> 00:09:37.000
You say: wait, wait, wait, no, the first one.

59
00:09:37.000 --> 00:09:41.000
No, no, by all characteristics this is definitely a second child.

60
00:09:41.000 --> 00:09:46.500
As you can see, there's no real euphoria when it's born, you get less shaken when it gets sick, it wears hand-me-down clothes and plays with battered toys: definitely a second child.

61
00:09:51.000 --> 00:09:57.000
Because it's true: when the first child is born, you go out, drink for ten days, drag music and a philharmonic around with you, torment the whole city.

62
00:09:57.000 --> 00:10:01.000
The second one is born, maybe an aunt writes on Facebook: became an aunt, and that's it.

63
00:10:01.000 --> 00:10:06.000
The first child gets sick, it's a medical council, you take its temperature all night, medicine, you keep vigil.

64
00:10:07.000 --> 00:10:11.000
The second one gets sick: has color in the face, fine, fine, fine.

65
00:10:11.000 --> 00:10:19.000
The second one you treat from a forum.

66
00:10:19.000 --> 00:10:25.000
You know, you wrap it in a little plantain leaf and just tie it with good string and lower it into some pond.

67
00:10:27.000 --> 00:10:31.000
I'm the oldest child. As a little kid I had so much attention,

68
00:10:31.000 --> 00:10:35.000
I have 3000 photos from when I was little, I'm in 2000 of them.

69
00:10:37.000 --> 00:10:41.000
My younger brother has three photos, and in one we're not sure if it's him.

70
00:10:41.000 --> 00:10:47.000
Grandma is holding something, is it him, is it a kitten, he was pretty ugly as a baby, we're not sure at all.

71
00:10:47.000 --> 00:10:50.000
And that can't help but affect your psyche.

72
00:10:50.000 --> 00:10:53.500
So, I'm used to attention, now I stand in front of you, talk here, stand in front of cameras, babble,

73
00:10:53.500 --> 00:10:57.000
and my younger brother, a complete heroin addict. A tough story.

74
00:10:57.000 --> 00:11:00.000
I'm kidding, he's a web designer, I'm just ashamed to say it.

75
00:11:05.000 --> 00:11:08.000
It would be easier for me to say he's on drugs than what he actually does.

76
00:11:15.000 --> 00:11:18.000
How the youngest one is even alive, we have no idea. Nobody watched over her.

77
00:11:22.000 --> 00:11:24.000
We have no idea how she's alive.

78
00:11:24.000 --> 00:11:32.000
When she was about three or four, Dad took her to a basketball game, because where else do you go with a three-year-old but the stands.

79
00:11:32.000 --> 00:11:41.000
And at one point he says: Ivana, what are you chewing? She says: gum. Ivana, where did you get gum?

80
00:11:42.000 --> 00:11:52.000
She peeled gum off a chair in the sports hall and is chewing it. So now she's immortal.

81
00:12:04.000 --> 00:12:12.000
By the way, I now have a second child. For quite a long time I had one child and now I have a second. The first was a girl, the second is a boy, and now it's agony raised to a completely new level.

82
00:12:12.000 --> 00:12:21.000
Because girls are emperors: a girl, as soon as she's born, is independent, she wants to learn everything, she wants to do everything herself, a girl looks life in the eye.

83
00:12:22.000 --> 00:12:31.000
A boy, a fat blob of jelly, sits there and his eyes say: you got me into this, so you'll support me.

84
00:12:31.000 --> 00:12:34.000
From today on I'm your concern.

85
00:12:35.000 --> 00:12:37.000
Don't expect too much from me.

86
00:12:37.000 --> 00:12:41.000
He's neither interested in anything nor knows anything, when you ask where the smart head is, he points at his sister.

87
00:12:51.000 --> 00:12:58.000
We tried to teach him to drink juice through a straw, and then he takes the straw, puts it in his mouth and raises his eyebrow.

88
00:12:59.000 --> 00:13:02.000
Because that's surely how you drink: you don't suck with your mouth, you raise your eyebrow.

89
00:13:03.000 --> 00:13:09.000
And then he's angry that it doesn't work. It's not him, it's that the straw is no good. The straw is no good if it doesn't come with an eyebrow raise, how else is he supposed to draw the juice?

90
00:13:17.000 --> 00:13:21.000
And something they still haven't warned me about.

91
00:13:21.000 --> 00:13:25.000
When you have a girl: that baby boys have erections. How awkward that is.

92
00:13:25.000 --> 00:13:28.000
You unwrap the diaper and the little antenna is standing up. Why at Dad, son?

93
00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:33.000
And then you can't put the diaper back, because it's standing.

94
00:13:33.000 --> 00:13:35.000
Then you have to bend it as if I'm playing Super Mario with a joystick.

95
00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:04.000
Anyway, when you have a brother or sister, that's competition, kind of healthy competition.

96
00:14:05.000 --> 00:14:10.000
When I was a kid, I didn't plan to do stand-up comedy, I'll tell you right away.

97
00:14:10.000 --> 00:14:13.000
As a little kid I was talented at writing little songs, little stories,

98
00:14:15.000 --> 00:14:19.000
And my dad kept comparing me to Bane, who was a better athlete than me: how Bane manages everything, how Bane...

99
00:14:19.000 --> 00:14:25.000
and Bane lives in the same building, my age, he on the first floor, me on the fourth, and all the time: how Bane manages both school and sports, how Bane.

100
00:14:25.000 --> 00:14:29.000
And really, Bane was faster and stronger and more agile than me.

101
00:14:29.000 --> 00:14:33.000
When we did the long jump in PE, we all jumped into the sand, Bane jumped over the sand.

102
00:14:38.000 --> 00:14:40.000
But I didn't give up on my dreams.

103
00:14:40.000 --> 00:14:45.000
I worked on myself, I improved myself.

104
00:14:45.000 --> 00:14:47.000
When I grew up, I got an award for the best young satirist in Serbia,

105
00:14:47.000 --> 00:14:49.000
I come all happy to my dad to rub it in his face,

106
00:14:49.000 --> 00:14:50.500
Bane signed for Chelsea.

107
00:14:50.500 --> 00:14:54.000
He'll torment me with this my whole life.

108
00:14:59.000 --> 00:15:03.000
I'm talking about Branislav Ivanović, the former captain of the Serbian national football team.

109
00:15:03.000 --> 00:15:08.000
My dad and his dad are friends. His dad is a very modest and nice man.

110
00:15:08.000 --> 00:15:14.000
And once: Bane scored two goals against Liverpool, my dad says to his: hey Rade, Bane scored two goals against Liverpool.

111
00:15:27.000 --> 00:15:35.000
I gave an interview recently and she says: you're now the most recognizable person in Sremska Mitrovica. I said: not even close, for a start there's Bane Ivanović.

112
00:15:35.000 --> 00:15:37.000
She says: well at least in the neighborhood.

113
00:15:38.000 --> 00:15:41.000
And she, trying to be funny, says: well at least in the building.

114
00:15:43.000 --> 00:15:49.000
These are the kinds of pressures that are hard to fight.

115
00:15:49.000 --> 00:15:53.000
And my wife constantly puts pressure on me, gives me tasks I can't fulfill.

116
00:15:53.000 --> 00:15:55.000
Like, for example, anything.

117
00:16:13.000 --> 00:16:19.000
She loves me and adores sending me to the store, because she knows I won't come back with all the items she told me to buy.

118
00:16:19.000 --> 00:16:23.000
And one day she says: Srđan, can I send you to the store? I say: Tamara, of course you can,

119
00:16:23.000 --> 00:16:26.000
she says: but you surely won't mess anything up, it's really urgent for me. I say: Tamara, I'm not an idiot.

120
00:16:26.000 --> 00:16:30.000
She says: are you sure? She says: here's the list.

121
00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:35.000
I say: fine, give me the list. She says: please, if something on the list isn't clear, there's a lady working there, ask her.

122
00:16:35.000 --> 00:16:39.000
I said: Tamara, come on, leave me alone, give me the list, I can do this.

123
00:16:39.000 --> 00:16:42.500
And not only did I not do it, I didn't even show up at home that evening.

124
00:16:43.000 --> 00:16:48.000
It all started completely naively. I set off, I really decide to do everything properly,

125
00:16:48.000 --> 00:16:53.000
but like Pinocchio meeting the cat and the fox on the way to school, I meet my friends, they're drinking some beer.

126
00:16:53.000 --> 00:16:59.000
And they say: come on, have a beer. I say: no, no, no, I have a really difficult task.

127
00:16:59.000 --> 00:17:01.000
They say: come on, one beer, nothing will happen. I said: okay, one beer.

128
00:17:01.000 --> 00:17:07.000
Seven beers later, with my peripheral vision I see a silhouette on the right.

129
00:17:09.000 --> 00:17:13.000
And now, I don't turn around suddenly so she doesn't sense fear, that's the worst, if she senses fear.

130
00:17:16.000 --> 00:17:20.000
I turn slowly, buying time to say the smartest thing possible.

131
00:17:39.000 --> 00:17:44.000
And now, I know I messed up, but I drank seven beers.

132
00:17:59.000 --> 00:18:03.000
She says: Srđan, believe me, not only will there be no sex tonight, but maybe never again.

133
00:18:03.000 --> 00:18:07.000
At that moment a cigarette lights up in the dark, local slackers are sitting on a bench and one guy speaks up.

134
00:18:22.000 --> 00:18:27.000
That's all from me for tonight, thank you very much for listening.
